  "description": "A 300 kg probe in a 35 km orbit over the poles of asteroid 433 Eros, whose gravity comes from its NEAR shape model. A 30 deg, 1024 px camera sees 100 landmarks on the surface; the tool's Kalman filter estimates the orbit from their pixels. The filter starts 100 m off (1 sigma). 2 days.\n\nWhat to look at:\n- probe-1.landmark_navigation.position_error: 5.5 m after the first image, 0.35 m median after, up to 4.8 m just after the stretches over the night side, inside the filter's own 3-sigma.\n- probe-1.landmark_navigation.landmarks_seen: about 18 landmarks per image; none over the night side (22 of 289 images).\n- probe-1.landmark_navigation.residual_rms: 0.28 px, the rounding to whole pixels.\n- probe-1.small_body.distance: the shape's pull moves the probe between 35.0 and 38.0 km from the centre.\n\nTry changing:\n- Added pixel noise (probe-1's Landmark navigation) to 1 px: the residuals grow to about 1 px and the error to about 5 m (median over the first half day).\n\nNote:\n- Needs the Eros files: Reference data, Download..., Small bodies. Basilisk's camera does not check whether terrain hides a landmark. About 4 minutes to run.",
